/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 1091 0 R
/T (The unfiltered Simulink model described in Chapter 3 is simulated for a duration sufficient to reach steady state. The simulation is then continued for several additional fundamental cycles to enable a clean FFT analysis. The AC supply current waveform obtained in the steady state is shown in Fig. 4.1. The waveform is markedly different from a pure sinusoid: it exhibits sharp, narrow peaks of opposite polarity that coincide with the conduction intervals of the bridge rectifier, separated by intervals of essentially zero current. This pulse-like shape is characteristic of a single-phase diode-bridge rectifier feeding a strongly inductive DC load. )
/E (The unfiltered Simulink model described in Chapter 3 is simulated for a duration sufficient to reach steady state. The simulation is then continued for several additional fundamental cycles to enable a clean FFT analysis. The AC supply current waveform obtained in the steady state is shown in Fig. 4.1. The waveform is markedly different from a pure sinusoid: it exhibits sharp, narrow peaks of opposite polarity that coincide with the conduction intervals of the bridge rectifier, separated by intervals of essentially zero current. This pulse-like shape is characteristic of a single-phase diode-bridge rectifier feeding a strongly inductive DC load. )
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
1761 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 1091 0 R
/T (The recordedsource-current time series is processed using the Powergui FFT Analysis tool. Thefundamental frequency is set to 50 Hz, a window of 10 fundamental cycles is selected,and the maximum analysed frequency is set to 1 kHz to focus on the dominant low-orderharmonics. The resulting harmonic spectrum is shown in Fig. 4.2. )
/E (The recordedsource-current time series is processed using the Powergui FFT Analysis tool. Thefundamental frequency is set to 50 Hz, a window of 10 fundamental cycles is selected,and the maximum analysed frequency is set to 1 kHz to focus on the dominant low-orderharmonics. The resulting harmonic spectrum is shown in Fig. 4.2. )
/Pg 25 0 R
