/T (4.6 Filter Integration )
/E (4.6 Filter Integration )
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
2100 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 1094 0 R
/T (The three single-tuned filter branches are connected in parallel and inserted in shunt at the AC side of the rectifier, immediately downstream of the transformer secondary. Each branch presents a low impedance only at its tuned frequency, so that the three branches collectively divert the 3rd, 5th and 7th harmonic currents back through the filter bank instead of forcing them through the source impedance. The modified Simulink model is shown in Fig. 4.3. )
/E (The three single-tuned filter branches are connected in parallel and inserted in shunt at the AC side of the rectifier, immediately downstream of the transformer secondary. Each branch presents a low impedance only at its tuned frequency, so that the three branches collectively divert the 3rd, 5th and 7th harmonic currents back through the filter bank instead of forcing them through the source impedance. The modified Simulink model is shown in Fig. 4.3. )
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
2101 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 1094 0 R
/T (Care is taken to ensure that, with the filter capacitances connected, the system does not present a parallel resonance with the source inductance near any of the dominant harmonic frequencies. Numerical sweeps of the equivalent source-side impedance confirmed that all parallel-resonance peaks fall well away from 150 Hz, 250 Hz and 350 Hz, validating the chosen capacitance values. )
/E (Care is taken to ensure that, with the filter capacitances connected, the system does not present a parallel resonance with the source inductance near any of the dominant harmonic frequencies. Numerical sweeps of the equivalent source-side impedance confirmed that all parallel-resonance peaks fall well away from 150 Hz, 250 Hz and 350 Hz, validating the chosen capacitance values. )
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
