6286 0 obj
<<
/Title (The rectifier serves as the interface between the AC supply system and the DC traction motor. Due to the nonlinear switching action of the diodes, the rectifier draws a non- sinusoidal current from the AC source, resulting in harmonic distortion in the supply current waveform. These harmonics are the primary source of power quality issues investigated in this project.)
/Dest [22 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 6284 0 R
/Next 6289 0 R
>>
endobj
6287 0 obj
<<
/Title (The mechanical traction load is represented by the Maxon RE6538898970 permanent magnet DC motor. This motor is widely used in laboratory-scale traction emulation rigs because its catalogue parameters \(rated voltage, no-load speed, stall torque, armature inductance and resistance\) are readily available and accurately documented by the manufacturer. The motor specifications used in the simulation are summarised in Table 3.1.)
/Dest [22 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 6289 0 R
/First 6290 0 R
/Last 6291 0 R
/Count -3
/Next 6285 0 R
>>
endobj
6288 0 obj
<<
/Title (script)
/Dest [23 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 6285 0 R
>>
endobj
6289 0 obj
<<
/Title (To improve the quality of the DC output, a series smoothing choke is connected at the rectifier output. The smoothing choke reduces current ripple supplied to the PMDC motor and promotes continuous current operation. Under these operating conditions, lower-order harmonics such as the 3rd, 5th, and 7th harmonics become dominant components ofthe source current spectrum, which were subsequently analyzed using FFT techniquesand mitigated using passive harmonic filters.  3.5 DC Motor)
/Dest [22 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 6286 0 R
/Next 6287 0 R
>>
endobj
